a/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv', ['home_team'])\nprint(\"\\nCup count:\", len(d2['home_team']))\nprint(sorted(d2['home_team'])[:40])\nEOF","time":{"start":1788226534027,"end":1788226534030}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The SDK is straightforward: create `MCPServer`, register tools with `@server.tool()`, call `server.run(\"stdio\")`.\n\nNow let me design the implementation:\n\n**Package structure:**\n```\nbrazilian_soccer_mcp/\n  __init__.py\n  __main__.py        # python -m brazilian_soccer_mcp\n  server.py          # MCP server with tools\n  data_loader.py     # loads + normalizes the 6 CSVs\n  normalize.py       # team name normalization, date parsing\n  queries.py         # query engine: matches, teams, players, competitions, stats\ntests/\n  test_normalize.py\n  test_data_loader.py\n  test_queries.py    # BDD-style scenarios\n  test_server.py     # MCP tool wiring\nrequirements.txt\npyproject.toml (maybe just requirements + simple layout)\n```\n\n**Data model:** normalize every match into a unified `Match` record:\n- date (ISO date), competition (\"Brasileirão\", \"Copa do Brasil\", \"Libertadores\", or tournament name from BR-Football), season, round/stage, home_team (normalized), away_team, home_goals, away_goals, home_team_state, away_team_state, arena, source_file.\n\n**Team name normalization:**\n- Strip state suffixes like \"-SP\", \"-RJ\" (only when a valid state abbreviation).\n- Handle country suffixes like \"Nacional (URU)\", \"Barcelona-EQU\".\n- Canonical aliases: map common variations (\"Athletico-PR\"/\"Athletico Paranaense\"/\"Atlético-PR\" → \"Athletico Paranaense\", \"Vasco\" → \"Vasco da Gama\", \"Santos FC\" → \"Santos\", etc.)\n- Unicode normalization (strip accents) for matching; keep canonical display name with accents.\n- Case-insensitive matching.\n\nLet me check the distinct team names in each dataset to build a good alias map.\n\n**Date parsing:** handle \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\", \"2023-09-24\", \"29/03/2003\", possibly \"DD/MM/YYYY\". Use datetime with multiple formats.\n\n**Queries:**\n1. `search_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, limit=20)` — returns matches with head-to-head summary.\n2. `get_team_stats(team, competition=None, season=None, venue=None)` — W/D/L, GF/GA, win rate.\n3. `head_to_head(team_a, team_b)` — record between teams.\n4. `search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_overall=None, limit=20)`.\n5. `get_standings(competition, season)` — computed from round-robin matches (filter to league competitions with enough matches; Brasileirão).\n6. `get_competition_stats(competition=None, season=None)` — avg goals, home win rate, biggest wins.\n7. `list_teams()` maybe. `list_competitions()`.\n8. `get_last_meeting(team_a, team_b)` — could be part of head_to_head.\n\nThe spec says \"At least 20 sample questions can be answered\". I should write tests covering at least 20 sample questions from the spec.\n\nLet me look at the distinct team names per file to craft the normalization.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05a9b7a67001W7ANKnMHsl7yDy","sessionID":"ses_fa566fb65ffeJ8k4Q5FXGIEPJE","messageID":"msg_05a9b125f001bk18K34vYZfk0u"}}
