{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788226863060,"sessionID":"ses_fa566fb65ffeJ8k4Q5FXGIEPJE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"todowrite","callID":"call_125fe61349fd4397947eb145","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"todos":[{"content":"Scaffold package layout (normalize, data_loader, queries, server, __main__)","status":"in_progress","priority":"high"},{"content":"Implement normalization (teams, dates, values)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Implement data loader for 6 CSVs with dedup","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Implement query engine (matches, teams, players, standings, stats, derbies)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Implement MCP server with tools + __main__","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Write tests (unit + BDD scenarios + server wiring)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Run tests, lint, end-to-end MCP smoke test","status":"pending","priority":"high"}]},"output":"[\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Scaffold package layout (normalize, data_loader, queries, server, __main__)\",\n    \"status\": \"in_progress\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Implement normalization (teams, dates, values)\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Implement data loader for 6 CSVs with dedup\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Implement query engine (matches, teams, players, standings, stats, derbies)\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Implement MCP server with tools + __main__\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Write tests (unit + BDD scenarios + server wiring)\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Run tests, lint, end-to-end MCP smoke test\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  }\n]","metadata":{"todos":[{"content":"Scaffold package layout (normalize, data_loader, queries, server, __main__)","status":"in_progress","priority":"high"},{"content":"Implement normalization (teams, dates, values)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Implement data loader for 6 CSVs with dedup","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Implement query engine (matches, teams, players, standings, stats, derbies)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Implement MCP server with tools + __main__","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Write tests (unit + BDD scenarios + server wiring)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Run tests, lint, end-to-end MCP smoke test","status":"pending","priority":"high"}],"truncated":false},"title":"7 todos","time":{"start":1788226863055,"end":1788226863058}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Copa do Brasil rounds per season: 2012 has 6, 2013-2020 mostly 8, 2016 has 7, 2021 partial (4). Max round per season isn't reliably the final (2012: 6 rounds — the 2012 Copa do Brasil final was Palmeiras vs Coritiba... in this dataset rounds might not map directly). I'll not guess; expose round filtering and add a `stage` note. For the \"finals\" test I'll use Libertadores stage=\"final\".\n\nNow, implementation plan. Let me write the code.\n\n### Package layout\n\n```\nbrazilian_soccer_mcp/\n  __init__.py          (version, exports)\n  normalize.py         (text/date/team normalization)\n  data_loader.py       (Dataset class: matches, players, indexes, dedup)\n  queries.py           (QueryEngine with all query methods)\n  server.py            (MCP MCPServer wiring, tools)\n  __main__.py          (main entry)\ntests/\n  conftest.py          (shared engine fixture)\n  test_normalize.py\n  test_data_loader.py\n  test_queries.py      (20+ sample question scenarios, BDD-style naming)\n  test_server.py       (tool list + call through server)\nrequirements.txt\npyproject.toml? — simple; use requirements.txt. Maybe pyproject for package metadata. Keep it simple: requirements.txt only, run with `venv/bin/python -m brazilian_soccer_mcp`. Also add a console script? Not needed.\n```\n\n### normalize.py\n\n```python\nimport re, unicodedata\nfrom datetime import datetime\n\nSTATES = {\"SP\",\"RJ\",\"MG\",\"RS\",\"PR\",\"BA\",\"SC\",\"PE\",\"CE\",\"GO\",\"MT\",\"MS\",\"AL\",\"PA\",\"PI\",\"RN\",\"PB\",\"SE\",\"ES\",\"AC\",\"AP\",\"AM\",\"DF\",\"RO\",\"RR\",\"TO\"}\nCOUNTRIES = {\"URU\",\"ARG\",\"PAR\",\"EQU\",\"BOL\",\"CHI\",\"COL\",\"PER\",\"VEN\",\"CRC\",\"HON\",\"GUA\",\"PAN\",\"MEX\",\"USA\",\"CRC\"}  # suffix country codes seen in Libertadores\n\ndef strip_accents(s) -> str\ndef _clean_raw(name) -> str: collapse whitespace, strip\ndef strip_suffix(name): remove trailing \"-XX\" or \" - XX\" or \"(XX)\" where XX in STATES|COUNTRIES (case-insens)\nALIASES: dict accent-stripped-lower -> canonical\ndef normalize_team(name) -> canonical display\ndef canonical_key(name) -> accent-stripped lower of canonical\n```\n\nAlias table (accent-stripped, lowercase → canonical):\n- \"atletico-mg\", \"atletico mineiro\", \"atletico-mg\" → \"Atlético Mineiro\"\n- \"athletico-pr\", \"atletico-pr\", \"athletico paranaense\", \"atletico paranaense\", \"athletico\", \"atletico\" (bare, from Libertadores) → hmm \"Atletico\" bare could be Atletico Nacional (Colombian) or Atlético de Madrid... In Libertadores data, \"Athletico\" is the Brazilian club (they rebranded to \"Athletico Paranaense\"). \"Atlético Nacional\" appears full. So \"athletico\" → \"Athletico Paranaense\". \"atletico\" bare → ambiguous; keep as \"Atlético\" (no alias). Careful with strip_suffix on \"Atlético - PR\" style from cup: \"Atletico - PR\" → strips \" - PR\" → \"Atletico\" then alias → Athletico Paranaense. But wait: cup has \"Atlético - AC\" (Atlético Acreano), \"Atlético - BA\" (Atlético Baiano), \"Atlético - GO\" → Goianiense, \"Atlético - MG\" → Mineiro, \"Atlético - PR\" → Paranaense, \"Atletico - ES\" (Atlético Tubarão? no...). Since I strip the state suffix before aliasing, \"Atlético - AC\" → \"Atlético\" → would wrongly map to Athletico Paranaense if I alias bare \"atletico\". Solution: alias only \"athletico\" (exact bare) → Athletico Paranaense since only Libertadores has bare \"Athletico\" and that IS the Brazilian club. Keep bare \"atletico\" unaliased. But then \"Atletico - ES\" → \"Atletico\" stays \"Atlético\"... it's fine — small clubs, low impact.\n\nActually wait, Libertadores has \"Athletico\" and also \"Atlético-PR\"; both → \"Athletico Paranaense\". \n\n- \"atletico-go\", \"atletico goianiense\" → \"Atlético Goianiense\"\n- \"america-mg\", \"america mg\", \"america fc (minas gerais)\" → \"América Mineiro\"\n- \"america-rn\", \"america rn\", \"america fc natal\" → \"América de Natal\"\n- \"america\" bare → \"América\" (careful: cup \"América - MG\" stripped → \"América\" — that would map to América Mineiro? The state is known: \"América - MG\" → strip suffix \" - MG\" gives \"América\". Hmm, then alias bare \"america\" → \"América\"? That loses MG info and collides with \"América - RN\" → also \"América\". \n\nBetter approach: alias lookup on the FULL raw name (accent-stripped, lowercase, whitespace-collapsed) FIRST, then fall back to suffix-stripping, then alias again. I.e.:\n\n1. key = accent_strip(lower(clean(name))) — lookup in alias dict built from full-name variants.\n2. If not found, strip state/country suffix → key again → lookup alias.\n3. If not found, apply canonical display from alias if exists else title-preserve original (minus suffix).\n\nAlias keys include hyphen/space variants: \"america-mg\", \"america - mg\", \"america mg\", \"america-mg\" all normalize the same if I collapse \" - \" and \"-\" to single space? Simplest: normalize key by replacing non-alphanumeric with space and collapsing. So \"america-mg\" → \"america mg\". \"Nacional (URU)\" → \"nacional uru\". Then alias keys are like \"america mg\" → \"América Mineiro\", \"atletico pr\"/\"athletico pr\"/\"athletico paranaense\"/\"athletico\" → \"Athletico Paranaense\". And after suffix strip: \"america\" — bare alias not mapped, display \"América\"... but display should be from the alias of the pre-strip key? Hmm.\n\nRule refinement: when suffix-strip path succeeds via alias, use alias canonical. When it doesn't, use the suffix-stripped name as display (title case as-is, keeping accents from original text — so strip suffix from the ORIGINAL string, not the key). For \"América - RN\" — is \"america rn\" in alias? I'll add \"america rn\"/\"america-rn\"/\"america natal\"/\"america de natal\"/\"america football club natal\" → \"América de Natal\" (RN). And \"america mg\" → \"América Mineiro\". Bare \"america\" (e.g., Libertadores \"América de Cali\" is full; cup might have bare \"America\"?) — display as-is \"América\". Good.\n\nAlso \"csa\"/\"c sa\"? Brasileirao has \"Csa-AL\" → strip → \"Csa\" → alias \"csa\" → \"CSA\". novo has \"CSA\". BR has \"ASA\"/\"ASA AL\" — \"asa\" → \"ASA\"? BR \"ASA\" vs \"ASA AL\" — same club (Agremiação Sportiva Arapiraquense - AL). alias \"asa al\" → \"ASA\", \"asa\" → \"ASA\".\n\nKey aliases for major teams (used in standings cross-file consistency — critical!):\n- \"sao paulo\", \"sao paulo fc\", \"sao paulo futebol clube\" → \"São Paulo\"\n- \"gremio\" → \"Grêmio\"; \"avai\" → \"Avaí\"; \"ceara\" → \"Ceará\"; \"cuiaba\" → \"Cuiabá\"; \"vitoria\" → \"Vitória\"; \"goias\" → \"Goiás\"; \"nautico\" → \"Náutico\"; \"criciuma\" → \"Criciúma\"; \"coritiba\" → \"Coritiba\"; \"atletico pr\" variants above; \"vasco\" → \"Vasco da Gama\"; \"sport\" → \"Sport Recife\"; \"sport club do recife\" → \"Sport Recife\"; \"santos\" → \"Santos\"; \"flamengo\" → \"Flamengo\"; etc. (Most already match after accent-stripping; the canonical display just needs accents.)\n- \"red bull bragantino\" / \"bragantino\" → \"Red Bull Bragantino\"? novo probably lacks it (2003-2019 has \"Barueri\"? no Bragantino). Brasileirao has \"Red Bull Bragantino-SP\". BR dataset? likely \"Red Bull Bragantino\" or \"Bragantino\". alias \"bragantino\" → \"Red Bull Bragantino\". Hmm but historical \"Bragantino\" pre-RB era (2019 BR dataset Serie B?). Acceptable for demo.\n- \"athletico paranaense\" FIFA club name → matches alias.\n- \"ceara sporting club\" → \"Ceará\"; \"america fc (minas gerais)\" → key \"america fc minas gerais\" → \"América Mineiro\"; \"parana\" → \"Paraná\"; \"parana clube\"→\"Paraná\"; \"sport club do recife\" → \"Sport Recife\"; \"chapecoense\" → \"Chapecoense\"; \"joinville\" → \"Joinville\"; \"juventude\" → \"Juventude\"; \"figueirense\" → \"Figueirense\"; \"ponte preta\" → \"Ponte Preta\"; \"portuguesa\" → \"Portuguesa\"; \"santa cruz\" → \"Santa Cruz\"; \"botafogo\" → \"Botafogo\" (careful \"Botafogo PB\" — key \"botafogo pb\" distinct, no alias); \"bahia\" → \"Bahia\"; \"fortaleza\" → \"Fortaleza\"; \"palmeiras\" → \"Palmeiras\"; \"corinthians\" → \"Corinthians\"; \"cruzeiro\" → \"Cruzeiro\"; \"atletico mineiro\" → \"Atlético Mineiro\"; \"internacional\" → \"Internacional\"; \"atletico goianiense\" → \"Atlético Goianiense\"; \"barueri\" → \"Barueri\"; \"brasiliense\" → \"Brasiliense\"; \"guarani\" → \"Guarani\"; \"ipatinga\" → \"Ipatinga\"; \"nautico\" → \"Náutico\"; \"paysandu\" → \"Paysandu\"; \"santo andre\" → \"Santo André\"; \"sao caetano\" → \"São Caetano\"; \"gremio prudente\" → \"Grêmio Prudente\"; \"atletico ac\"→\"Atlético Acreano\"? skip.\n\nThe display name should carry proper accents. The alias maps key→canonical display; if a team's key isn't in aliases, display = original minus suffix (preserving original accents), and key = accent-stripped. So matching across files works even for unmapped names (accent+case-insensitive + suffix-stripped).\n\nEdge: \"Vasco da Gama-RJ\" → key \"vasco da gama\" → alias → \"Vasco da Gama\". novo \"Vasco\" → alias \"vasco\" → \"Vasco da Gama\". \n\nDerbies list (canonical pairs, order-insensitive):\n- Fla-Flu: Flamengo/Fluminense\n- Clássico dos Milhões: Flamengo/Vasco da Gama\n- Clássico Vovô: Botafogo/Fluminense? (also \"Clássico dos Milhões\" is Fla-Vasco; \"Clássico dos Apaixonados\"? skip minor ones) — include Botafogo vs Flamengo (\"Clássico da Rivalidade\")? and Botafogo vs Vasco (\"Clássico da Amizade\")? Keep the four RJ rivalries.\n- Choque-Rei: Palmeiras/São Paulo\n- Majestoso: Corinthians/São Paulo\n- Dérbi Paulista: Corinthians/Palmeiras\n- Gre-Nal: Grêmio/Internacional\n- Ba-Vi: Bahia/Vitória\n- Atletiba: Athletico Paranaense/Coritiba\n- Clássico-Rei: Ceará/Fortaleza\n- Clássico das Multidões? skip. \n- Jaguarem? skip. That's enough (10 derbies).\n\nDate parsing formats: \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\", \"%Y-%m-%d\", \"%d/%m/%Y\", \"%d/%m/%Y %H:%M:%S\". Also handle empty/NA → None.\n\nValue parsing for FIFA (\"€110.5M\", \"€565K\") → numeric euros for sorting maybe; nice touch: parse to float.\n\n### data_loader.py\n\nDataclasses:\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass Match: date, season, competition, round, stage, home_team, away_team, home_goals, away_goals, home_state, away_state, arena, source\n@dataclass\nclass Player: id, name, age, nationality, overall, potential, club, position, jersey_number, height, weight, preferred_foot, value, wage, skills: dict\n```\n\nDataset class:\n- loads 6 files\n- competition assignment: \"Brasileirão\" (Brasileirao_Matches + novo_campeonato), \"Copa do Brasil\" (cup + BR tournament Copa do Brasil), \"Libertadores\", \"Serie A\"/\"Serie B\"/\"Serie C\" (BR). Hmm — should BR \"Copa do Brasil\" merge with cup file? Yes, same competition. BR \"Serie A\" — is it the same as \"Brasileirão\"? For 2014-2023 overlap with Brasileirao_Matches (2012-2022): dedup needed. Merge strategy: canonical competition classes:\n  - \"Brasileirão\" ← Brasileirao_Matches, novo_campeonato, BR \"Serie A\"\n  - \"Copa do Brasil\" ← Cup file, BR \"Copa do Brasil\"\n  - \"Libertadores\"\n  - BR \"Serie B\"/\"Serie C\" stay separate.\n  \n  But wait — \"Serie B\" from BR might be useful; keep competition as \"Serie B\" distinct. However display in answers: \"Brasileirão\". Round-robin standings need consistent round counts; dedup by (date, home, away, league_class) handles overlap. Priority for dedup: prefer record with arena+state (novo) > Brasileirao_Matches (state) > BR. For cup class: cup file > BR.\n\n  One concern: BR Serie A rounds — does BR dataset have a round column? No (columns: tournament,home,away,goals,corner,attack,shots,time,date,ht,at,total_corners). OK.\n\n  Dedup key: (comp_class, date, home_key, away_key). But same league on same date with identical pair twice? Extremely unlikely (double round robin = one match per pairing per venue per season). Use (comp_class, season, date, home_key, away_key).\n\n- Also aggregate seasons list, competitions list, teams list (with counts), derby detection, h2h index maybe (just compute on demand — 24k matches scan is fast enough; but for perf, build an index dict team->list of match idx. With <2s simple lookups required, plain scans of ~24k rows are ~ms in Python. Players 18k scan fine. No need for heavy indexing, but I'll build a team->matches index for speed and simplicity.)\n\n- Players: load with csv, parse subset of columns. Value/Wage parse to numeric.\n\nEmpty goals → None; matches with None goals excluded from stats/standings but included in schedule searches.\n\n### queries.py — QueryEngine\n\nMethods (all return dicts/str — will be JSON for MCP):\n- `search_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, round_or_stage=None, limit=20, include_summary=True)`:\n  - team: matches where team is home or away (normalized). opponent: matches against opponent (if only opponent given, treat as team).\n  - competition: fuzzy comp match (\"brasileirao\", \"serie a\", \"copa do brasil\", \"libertadores\", \"serie b\", \"serie c\").\n  - date_from/date_to: parse via date parser (accept YYYY-MM-DD or DD/MM/YYYY).\n  - summary: h2h between team & opponent if both, else team W/D/L in the filtered set.\n  - Return: {\"matches\": [...], \"total\": n, \"returned\": k, \"summary\": {...}} with match lines formatted \"2023-09-03: Flamengo 2-1 Fluminense (Brasileirão, Round 22)\".\n- `get_team_stats(team, competition=None, season=None, venue=None)`: W/D/L, GF/GA, win rate, per venue. venue in {home, away, all}.\n- `head_to_head(team_a, team_b)`: overall record, last meetings list (most recent 5), each match.\n- `search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, position_category=None, min_overall=None, max_overall=None, limit=20)`: name substring (case/diacritic-insensitive); club matching with rank (exact > prefix > contains) and return matched club name; position: match either FIFA position exactly or in category set. Nationality fuzzy (e.g., \"Brazil\" exact; \"brazilian\" → \"Brazil\"? add nationality alias: strip trailing \"ian/an/ese\"? too fuzzy — handle \"brazil\"→\"Brazil\" via contains. \"Brazilian\" contains \"brazil\" — match if nationality equals query or query contains nationality lower or nationality contains query? \"brazilian\" contains \"brazil\" — so filter nationality where norm(query) in norm(nat) or norm(nat) in norm(query). That handles \"brazilian\"→\"brazil\".)\n  - Sort by overall desc default when no query? Default sort: name asc; if min_overall or top-style, sort overall desc. I'll sort by overall desc, then name — for \"find all Brazilian players\" example format they show sorted by rating. Good: always sort overall desc unless name query (then name match relevance). Simple: sort by (overall desc, name) always; name search keeps that too (fine).\n  - position_category sets: forward {ST, CF, LW, RW, LS, RS, LF, RF}, midfielder {CAM, LAM, RAM, CM, LCM, RCM, LM, RM, CDM, LDM, RDM}, defender {LB, LWB, CB, LCB, RCB, RB, RWB}, goalkeeper {GK}.\n- `get_standings(competition, season)`: league matches for that season (comp class must be \"Brasileirão\"/\"Serie A\"-merged... standings for Serie B too if asked, but Brasileirão is the main). Filter: goals not None. Compute table: P, W, D, L, GF, GA, GD, Pts. Sort Pts, W, GD, GF. Champion = row 1 (only if table complete — say ≥ half of teams played ≥ (n_teams-1) matches? Keep simple: require at least (n-1) matches per team where n = number of teams? For 2019 with dedup across 3 sources the table should be complete 38 rounds × ... let me not over-engineer: compute table, mark \"Champion\" on top row. Relegation: mark bottom 4 if exactly 20 teams. Also provide \"is_complete\" heuristic: each team played same count? I'll include matches_count and a note.\n- `get_competition_stats(competition=None, season=None, top=5)`: n_matches, avg goals/match, home win %, draw %, away win %, biggest wins (top 5 by margin), top scoring teams.\n- `get_best_records(scope=\"home\"|\"away\", competition=None, season=None, limit=5)`: teams ranked by win rate (min matches filter e.g. ≥5). Covers \"Which team has the best away record?\" / \"best home record\".\n- `search_derbies(season=None, competition=None)`: matches where (home,away) in derby set.\n- `get_team_competitions(team)`: comps + seasons played.\n- `get_club_overview(team)`: team stats all-time + per-season recent + players at club (FIFA) — cross-file.\n- `list_teams(competition=None, limit=None)`: teams with match counts.\n- `list_competitions()`: comps with match counts and season range.\n- `list_seasons(competition=None)`.\n- `get_season_summary(season)` and `compare_seasons(a, b)`: matches, goals, avg, home win rate, champion per comp.\n\nThat's comprehensive. 20+ sample questions covered.\n\nMCP tools (server.py) — register the above; concise docstrings with param descriptions. Tool names: search_matches, get_team_stats, head_to_head, search_players, get_standings, get_competition_stats, get_best_records, search_derbies, get_team_competitions, get_club_overview, list_teams, list_competitions, list_seasons, compare_seasons. That's 14 — maybe merge list_seasons into list_competitions (include season ranges). Yes: list_competitions returns per-comp seasons. Drop list_seasons → 13 tools. Also drop compare_seasons (get_season_summary × 2 via LLM). Keep get_season_summary. → 12 tools:\n\n1. search_matches\n2. get_team_stats\n3. head_to_head\n4. get_last_meeting? — head_to_head includes last meetings. Skip.\n5. search_players\n6. get_standings\n7. get_competition_stats\n8. get_best_records\n9. search_derbies\n10. get_team_competitions\n11. get_club_overview\n12. list_teams\n13. list_competitions\n14. get_season_summary\n\n13 tools. Fine.\n\nResources: could add a resource \"brazilian-soccer://summary\" with dataset overview. Nice touch — add `dataset://overview` resource and instructions. Keep minimal but real.\n\n### server.py with mcp 2.x\n\n```python\nfrom mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer\nserver = MCPServer(name=\"brazilian-soccer\", instructions=...)\n@server.tool()\ndef search_matches(team: str | None = None, ...) -> dict: ...\n```\n\nNote mcp 2.x tool decorator signature (name, title, description...). Docstrings become descriptions presumably.\n\nData path: resolve relative to repo root: Path(__file__).parent.parent / \"data\" / \"kaggle\", overridable via env BRAZILIAN_SOCCER_DATA_DIR. Dataset loads lazily (on first tool call) to keep import fast, or eagerly at startup? Eager at server start is fine (<2s). I'll load lazily with a module-level singleton via `get_dataset()`.\n\n### Tests (pytest)\n\n- test_normalize.py: team normalization cases (state suffix, country suffix, accents, aliases, case), date parsing (all formats), value parsing.\n- test_data_loader.py: all 6 files load; row counts > 0; unified match fields; dedup works; empty goals → None; players loaded 18207.\n- test_queries.py: BDD-style — I'll use pytest with descriptive names + given/when/then comments. Cover the 20+ sample questions.\n- test_server.py: list_tools shows all tools; call_tool through the server's manager? For mcp 2.x, I can test via `server.call_tool` async? Let me check MCPServer.call_tool signature — it's likely an async method used internally. Simpler: test that tools registered by calling `await server.list_tools()` using anyio. Let me check the API at build time.\n\nAlso `python -m brazilian_soccer_mcp` should start stdio server. And I'll write a quick end-to-end smoke test speaking JSON-RPC over stdio? That's heavier; a lighter approach: use mcp client with memory transport? mcp 2.x client has in-memory transports? In 1.x there was `create_connected_server_and_client_session`. Let me check what's available in 2.1.1. I'll check during implementation. At minimum, test server.tool registration and direct function calls.\n\nLet me also check MCPServer.call_tool / list_tools signatures.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05aa07fc8001hf7VI7BDiIHFri","sessionID":"ses_fa566fb65ffeJ8k4Q5FXGIEPJE","messageID":"msg_05a9dfdd3001Nho7quDPTOFRFs"}}
