{2}$', n))\nprint(\"\\nHyphen-suffixed:\", hyph)\nEOF","time":{"start":1788227027665,"end":1788227027667}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Wait, I made a mistake — I put `\"brazil\": \"Seleção Brasileira\"` in the alias table as a joke comment. That's wrong: \"brazil\" would never be a team name in match data, but it's sloppy. Let me remove that entry.\n\nAlso, there's a subtle problem: \"nacional\" → \"Nacional\" and \"nacional uru\" → \"Nacional\" — but there's also Colombian \"Atlético Nacional\" (mapped) and Brazilian Nacional clubs... fine for demo. Actually wait, mapping \"nacional uru\" → \"Nacional\" loses country distinction, but Libertadores only has one Nacional (URU), so OK.\n\nHmm, also \"junior de barranquilla\" → \"Junior\" — fine.\n\nAnother issue: `_SUFFIX_RE` with `[-–(]+` — for \"Nacional (URU)\": base=\"Nacional\", code=\"URU\" ∈ countries → good. For \"América - MG\": matches \" - MG\" → good. For \"Csa-AL\" → \"Csa\",\"AL\". For \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" — the regex `^(.+?)\\s*[-–(]+\\s*([A-Za-z]{2,3})\\s*\\)?$` — the `.+?` is non-greedy but anchored `$`, so it will try to match from earliest possible position... Actually non-greedy + `$` anchor: it finds the FIRST split that satisfies the pattern ending at end-of-string. \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\": the regex needs base then `[-–(]+` then code then optional `)`. Candidate splits: after \"Club\" comes \"(\" then \"antigo\" — \"antigo\" is 6 letters, not ≤3, fail at that position but regex will backtrack to next split point... Actually with `.+?` expanding, it tries: base=\"Boavista Sport Club\", sep=\"(\", code must be [A-Za-z]{2,3} then optional \")\" then $ — \"antigo\" starts with \"ant\" (3 letters) then requires \\s*\\)?$ — after \"ant\" comes \"igo...\" which is not `\\s*\\)?$`, fail; try 2-letter \"an\" then \"tigo\" ≠ end → fail; continue expanding base... base=\"...Barreira)\", sep=\" - \", code=\"RJ\", then `\\s*\\)?$` → end. So base = \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira)\". Good, that works via backtracking.\n\nBut wait — there's a bug: after the match, I do `base.strip(\" -–(\")`. For \"Nacional (URU)\" base would be \"Nacional (\"? No: for \"Nacional (URU)\": non-greedy base=\"Nacional\", sep=\"(\", code=\"URU\", `\\)?$` matches \")\" at end. base=\"Nacional\" clean. For a hypothetical \"Club (AB\" unbalanced — fine.\n\nEdge: \"A.b.c. - RN\" — base \"A.b.c.\" — strip(\" -–(\") — trailing \".\" stays. key \"a b c\" hmm text_key(\"A.b.c.\") → \"a b c\". Not in aliases; display fallback title-case: \"A.b.c.\". OK.\n\nEdge: \"Atlético - AC\" — \"Atlético\" base, code AC (state) → base \"Atlético\", key \"atletico\" not aliased (only \"athletico\") → display \"Atlético\". Good, avoids collision.\n\nEdge: \"Barcelona-EQU\" → \"Barcelona\" → alias \"barcelona equ\"? Wait — order of operations: I first check full key \"barcelona equ\" — is it in aliases? Yes, I added \"barcelona equ\" → \"Barcelona SC\". Good, checked BEFORE suffix strip. Similarly \"guaraní par\" → \"Guaraní\" via full-key alias. And \"nacional uru\" → \"Nacional\". \n\nBut careful: full-key check happens first, so \"America - MG\" full key \"america mg\" → alias hit → \"América Mineiro\" — good.\n\nOne more issue: `\"asa\"` alias → \"ASA\" and `\"as a\"` → \"ASA\" — the latter key came from \"A.S.A.\" → text_key → \"a s a\". I wrote \"as a\" (wrong — that'd be from \"as-a\"?). Should be \"a s a\". Let me fix: add \"a s a\" and \"a s a al\" keys. Also \"abc\" key: text_key(\"ABC\") → \"abc\" ✓. \"Abc - RN\" full key \"abc rn\" ✓ aliased.\n\nAlso missing: \"americano rj\"? BR has \"Americano RJ\" — full key \"americano rj\", no alias; suffix-strip → \"Americano\" display. Fine.\n\n\"Aguia Negra-MS\" — suffix-strip → \"Aguia Negra\" → alias \"aguia negra\" → \"Águia Negra\" ✓ (I have it).\n\n\"Aparecidense GO\" (BR) — suffix strip → \"Aparecidense\" ✓ display preserved. But wait — the suffix regex requires `[-–(]+` separator; \"Aparecidense GO\" has a SPACE separator, not \"-\". So no strip → key \"aparecidense go\" not aliased → display \"Aparecidense GO\" — inconsistent with cup's \"Aparecidense - GO\" → \"Aparecidense\". Mismatch in canonical display for the same club (two variants). Match-level queries by \"Aparecidense\" then miss \"Aparecidense GO\". Solution: extend regex to allow space-only separator? Dangerous: \"Red Bull Bragantino\" — could \"Bull\" be read as code? No — code must be 2-3 letters and in the suffix set. \"Red Bull Bragantino\" ends with \"Bragantino\" (9 letters) → no match. \"Boa Vista\"? ends \"Vista\" no. What about \"América FC Natal\" — ends \"Natal\" (5) no. \"ASA AL\" → code \"AL\" in states, base \"ASA\" → alias → \"ASA\" ✓ (with space separator allowed). \"America MG\" (BR) → base \"America\" + code \"MG\"... but full key \"america mg\" hits alias first → \"América Mineiro\" ✓. \"America FC Natal\" — ends \"Natal\"? \"Natal\" is not a 2-3 letter code → no strip → key \"america fc natal\" → aliased → \"América de Natal\" ✓. \"Aguia de Maraba\" — \"Maraba\" no. \"Boavista RJ\" (BR) → full key \"boavista rj\" aliased ✓. \"Audax SP\" → space strip → \"Audax\" ✓ display. \"Anapolis FC\" — \"FC\" not a state code ✓ stays. \"Americano RJ\" → \"Americano\" ✓. \"Botafogo PB\" → \"PB\" is a state code! base \"Botafogo\" → WRONG — \"Botafogo PB\" (João Pessoa) would canonicalize to \"Botafogo\" (RJ). Danger! Same for \"America RN\"→handled by alias, but \"Botafogo PB\" has no alias and would wrongly become \"Botafogo\".\n\nMitigation: space-separated suffix stripping only when the base isn't otherwise known...? Cleaner: only allow SPACE separators for a whitelist of pattern \"X XX\" where X is multi-word/ambiguous? Hmm.\n\nAlternative: keep space-separator stripping but add explicit aliases for known collisions: \"botafogo pb\" → \"Botafogo-PB\". Are there other space-separated state-suffix names in BR dataset that collide with real clubs? \"America RN\" aliased ✓. \"Boavista RJ\" aliased ✓. \"ABC RN\"? BR had \"America FC Natal\"... Let me scan BR home/away names ending with \" XX\" state patterns to find collisions. Similarly \"Atletico GO\"? etc. Also \"Csa AL\"? \"ASA AL\" fine.\n\nAlso two-letter word endings that ARE states but not suffixes: \"Boa\" no... Let me grep the distinct names in BR dataset ending in a state code after space.\n\nThen add defensive aliases: \"botafogo pb\" → \"Botafogo-PB\". Also for safety map \"botafogo pb\" etc.\n\nAlso note: suffix regex with space separator would also hit \"Red Bull Bragantino-SP\"? that's hyphen. OK.\n\nAdditional consideration: `strip_team_suffix` is also used to extract state codes for home_state when datasets lack them. For BR dataset, names like \"Botafogo PB\" give state PB — that's actually correct usage there! The issue is only that normalize_team then displays \"Botafogo\". So: normalize_team should treat space-separated suffix stripping more carefully — apply alias on FULL key first (so \"botafogo pb\" if aliased wins), and only fall back to strip. With \"botafogo pb\" aliased to \"Botafogo-PB\", full-key lookup resolves before stripping. \n\nLet me scan BR dataset for names ending with space+state-code to catalog them and alias the colliding ones.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05aa3027e001x2T1pUlNyxhF82","sessionID":"ses_fa566fb65ffeJ8k4Q5FXGIEPJE","messageID":"msg_05aa1ca95001fiwaGeCD3MR4Ke"}}
