` builds an awkward generator. Let me rewrite it more clearly.\n\n4. In `search_players`, `name_key not in text_key(player.name)` — should also handle multi-word names in any order? e.g. \"Gabriel Barbosa\" vs FIFA \"Gabriel Barbosa\" (FIFA uses \"Gabriel Barbosa\"? or \"G. Barbosa\"?). FIFA data uses short names like \"L. Messi\". \"Gabriel Barbosa\" is likely full \"Gabriel Barbosa\". To be safe, support token-subset matching: all query tokens must appear in the player name key (in any order). That handles \"Barbosa Gabriel\" too. Let me implement: tokens of name_key ⊆ tokens of player name key → match; or substring fallback. I'll do: match if all tokens in name_key appear in player_name_key OR name_key is substring.\n\nAlso for \"Who is Gabriel Barbosa?\" — good.\n\n5. `_match_club` sets instance attrs `_last_club_match_quality` — but never initialized in __init__ and never used. Either use it in response or remove. I'll remove and instead return matched club names in the search_players response. Let me restructure: compute matched club names when club filter used and include in response as \"matched_clubs\".\n\n6. `get_standings` note: `matches_per_team` unused beyond complete check — fine.\n\n7. `derby_name` in queries uses normalize — fine.\n\n8. In `search_derbies` I import team_key — used ✓.\n\n9. `_filtered_record_summary` staticmethod referencing QueryEngine._record — fine.\n\n10. `get_team_competitions` — rewrite cleanly:\n\n```python\nby_comp: dict[str, set[int]] = defaultdict(set)\ncounts: dict[str, int] = defaultdict(int)\nfor match in matches:\n    counts[match.competition] += 1\n    if match.season:\n        by_comp[match.competition].add(match.season)\ncompetitions = {comp: {\"matches\": counts[comp], \"seasons\": sorted(by_comp[comp])} for comp in sorted(counts)}\n```\n\nLet me fix all these now.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05aa817c8001s5eQy4txYnMb7G","sessionID":"ses_fa566fb65ffeJ8k4Q5FXGIEPJE","messageID":"msg_05aa7a3d4001GjW9Ul3e