ames=set()\nfor f in files:\n    with open(f'data/kaggle/{f}', newline='', encoding='utf-8') as fh:\n        for r in csv.DictReader(fh):\n            for c in name_cols[f]:\n                if r.get(c): allnames.add(r[c].strip())\n\n# canonical display -> set of identity keys under strip rule\nSTRIP = {'fc','ec','sc','cf','clube','esporte clube','futebol clube','sport club','esporte','futebol','club'}\ndef ident(display):\n    k = text_key(display)\n    toks = k.split()\n    # strip up to 2 trailing generic tokens\n    while len(toks)>1 and ' '.join(toks[-2:]) in STRIP:\n        toks = toks[:-2] if ' '.join(toks[-2:]) in STRIP else toks\n    while toks and toks[-1] in {'fc','ec','sc','cf','club','clube','esporte','futebol'}:\n        toks.pop()\n    return ' '.join(toks)\n\ngroups=defaultdict(set)\nfor n in allnames:\n    disp = normalize_team(n)\n    groups[ident(disp)].add(disp)\ncollisions = {k:v for k,v in groups.items() if len(v)>1}\nprint(f\"{len(allnames)} raw names -> {len(groups)} identities, {len(collisions)} collisions:\")\nfor k,v in sorted(collisions.items()):\n    print(\" \", k, \"=>\", sorted(v))\nEOF","time":{"start":1788227700943,"end":1788227700947}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Brasileirao_Matches 2022 has 380 matches including \"Corinthians-SP\" — my grep failed because I checked `'Corinthians' in (r['home_team'], r['away_team'])`... \"Corinthians-SP\" — 'Corinthians' IS a substring of \"Corinthians-SP\"! `'Corinthians' in 'Corinthians-SP'` → True. Wait, that should have matched! Oh... I see — in the debug script I wrote the set comprehension over csv.DictReader for Brasileirao_Matches and filtered `r['season']=='2022' and 'Corinthians' in ...`. That should work. Unless... hmm, wait, actually let me re-look: \n\n```python\nbMatches.add((r['datetime'][:10], r['home_team'], r['away_team']))\n```\nand the filter — oh! I see it. In that script the filter was:\n```python\nif r['season']=='2022' and 'Corinthians' in (r['home_team'], r['away_team']):\n```\nHmm that's `in` on a tuple → checks membership of exact elements, NOT substring! `('Corinthians' in ('Corinthians-SP','X'))` → False because tuple membership compares whole elements! That was MY bug in the debug script, not the data. Embarrassing. OK so Brasileirao 2022 does have Corinthians-SP matches.\n\nSo the real dedup issue: BR names \"Fortaleza FC\" vs \"Fortaleza\", \"EC Juventude\" vs \"Juventude\", \"Atletico Mineiro\" vs \"Atletico-MG\"→\"Atlético Mineiro\" ✓ (alias), \"Atletico Goianiense\" → alias ✓, \"Botafogo RJ\" → alias ✓, \"Avai\" → alias? \"avai\" → \"Avaí\" ✓, \"Ceara\" → \"Ceará\" ✓ (alias), \"Bragantino\" → \"Red Bull Bragantino\" ✓ alias, \"Cuiaba\" → \"Cuiabá\" ✓, \"Sao Paulo\" → \"São Paulo\" ✓, \"America MG\" → full key alias ✓, \"Athletico Paranaense\" ✓, \"Goias\" ✓ alias, \"Coritiba\" ✓, \"Santos\" ✓.\n\nSo remaining mismatches: \"Fortaleza FC\" → normalize → \"Fortaleza FC\" (no alias!) ≠ \"Fortaleza\". \"EC Juventude\" → \"EC Juventude\" ≠ \"Juventude\". These two break dedup → duplicates (121 pts for Flamengo 2019 came from \"Fortaleza FC\" mismatch: Flamengo×Fortaleza games counted twice).\n\nSolution: add aliases \"fortaleza fc\" → \"Fortaleza\", \"ec juventude\" → \"Juventude\", and more generally implement a **generic fallback** in normalize_team: if the name (after suffix strip) ends with a generic club-type token (fc, ec, sc, ac, cf, clube, esporte clube, futebol clube), strip it and retry the alias map, and use the stripped name as display ONLY IF the alias lookup of the stripped key hits. Otherwise keep original. This is safe because display only changes when we KNOW the canonical name (alias hit). But for names not in alias (e.g. \"Anapolis FC\"), display stays \"Anapolis FC\" — mismatch risk remains for cross-file dedup (\"Anápolis\" from cup vs \"Anapolis FC\" from BR). That affects only small clubs in Serie B/C — acceptable for dedup of major-league data, but let me broaden slightly: also strip tokens and set identity/display from the stripped form when the stripped key appears in the KNOWN team universe? I don't have the universe at normalize time (normalize is pure). \n\nBetter: make `team_key` (identity) strip generic trailing tokens unconditionally, and make display resolution two-phase in the Dataset loader: after loading, for teams whose normalized display differs from others' identity... too complex.\n\nPragmatic: \n- identity_key: text_key(normalize) then drop trailing tokens from {fc, ec, sc, ac, cf, clube, esporte, futebol} (one or two trailing tokens like \"esporte clube\", \"futebol clube\", \"sport club\"? — careful \"Sport Club Internacional\"? not in data).\n- Collision guard: \"Rio Branco AC\" → \"rio branco\" collides with \"Rio Branco ES\" → \"rio branco\"! Both in Serie C/D data. Risky for dedup? They'd need same comp+season+date+opponent... different comps presumably. I'll drop \"ac\" from the strip list (Acre clubs) — keep {fc, ec, sc, cf}? \"SC\" — \"Remo SC\"? Hmm \"EC Internacional SC\"... Let me check which of these stripped identities actually collide in the real corpus, and only strip tokens that don't create collisions. I can compute this once here and then hardcode the safe strip list + explicit aliases for the rest.\n\nLet me compute: take all 5 match datasets' team names → normalized display → identity candidates with each strip rule → find collisions across distinct display names.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05aad4859001j2wl8oY7KMjfTd","sessionID":"ses_fa566fb65ffeJ8k4Q5FXGIEPJE","messageID":"msg_05aac9572001rHZlB0cP1e5BYZ"}}
