{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788227860867,"sessionID":"ses_fa566fb65ffeJ8k4Q5FXGIEPJE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_76b99230ed054813bad646f6","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-75154ofp/retort-0076cc65ba78/brazilian_soccer_mcp/normalize.py","newString":"def team_key(name: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Stable identity key for a team after canonicalization.\"\"\"\n    return text_key(normalize_team(name))\n\n\n# Generic club-type tokens ignored when computing a team's identity key, so\n# \"Fortaleza\", \"Fortaleza FC\", and \"Fortaleza EC\" share one identity.\n_GENERIC_TOKENS = {\"fc\", \"ec\", \"sc\", \"cf\", \"club\", \"clube\", \"esporte\",\n                   \"futebol\"}\n_GENERIC_PAIRS = {\"esporte clube\", \"futebol clube\", \"sport club\"}\n\n\ndef identity_key(name: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Identity used for cross-file dedup and team matching.\n\n    Same as team_key but additionally ignores generic club-type tokens:\n    \"Fortaleza FC\" and \"Fortaleza\" share the identity \"fortaleza\".\n    \"\"\"\n    tokens = team_key(name).split()\n    while len(tokens) >= 2 and \" \".join(tokens[-2:]) in _GENERIC_PAIRS:\n        del tokens[-2:]\n    while tokens and tokens[-1] in _GENERIC_TOKENS:\n        tokens.pop()\n    while tokens and tokens[0] in _GENERIC_TOKENS:\n        tokens.pop(0)\n    return \" \".join(tokens)","oldString":"def team_key(name: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Stable identity key for a team after canonicalization.\"\"\"\n    return text_key(normalize_team(name))"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-75154ofp/retort-0076cc65ba78/brazilian_soccer_mcp/normalize.py\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-75154ofp/retort-0076cc65ba78/brazilian_soccer_mcp/normalize.py\n+++ /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-75154ofp/retort-0076cc65ba78/brazilian_soccer_mcp/normalize.py\n@@ -251,8 +251,31 @@\n     \"\"\"Stable identity key for a team after canonicalization.\"\"\"\n     return text_key(normalize_team(name))\n \n \n+# Generic club-type tokens ignored when computing a team's identity key, so\n+# \"Fortaleza\", \"Fortaleza FC\", and \"Fortaleza EC\" share one identity.\n+_GENERIC_TOKENS = {\"fc\", \"ec\", \"sc\", \"cf\", \"club\", \"clube\", \"esporte\",\n+                   \"futebol\"}\n+_GENERIC_PAIRS = {\"esporte clube\", \"futebol clube\", \"sport club\"}\n+\n+\n+def identity_key(name: str) -> str:\n+    \"\"\"Identity used for cross-file dedup and team matching.\n+\n+    Same as team_key but additionally ignores generic club-type tokens:\n+    \"Fortaleza FC\" and \"Fortaleza\" share the identity \"fortaleza\".\n+    \"\"\"\n+    tokens = team_key(name).split()\n+    while len(tokens) >= 2 and \" \".join(tokens[-2:]) in _GENERIC_PAIRS:\n+        del tokens[-2:]\n+    while tokens and tokens[-1] in _GENERIC_TOKENS:\n+        tokens.pop()\n+    while tokens and tokens[0] in _GENERIC_TOKENS:\n+        tokens.pop(0)\n+    return \" \".join(tokens)\n+\n+\n def derby_name(team_a: str, team_b: str) -> str | None:\n     \"\"\"Return the rivalry name if two teams are a known derby, else None.\"\"\"\n     a, b = normalize_team(team_a), normalize_team(team_b)\n     pair = (a, b) if a < b else (b, a)\n","filediff":{"file":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-75154ofp/retort-0076cc65ba78/brazilian_soccer_mcp/normalize.py","patch":"Index: /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-75154ofp/retort-0076cc65ba78/brazilian_soccer_mcp/normalize.py\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-75154ofp/retort-0076cc65ba78/brazilian_soccer_mcp/normalize.py\n+++ /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-75154ofp/retort-0076cc65ba78/brazilian_soccer_mcp/normalize.py\n@@ -251,8 +251,31 @@\n     \"\"\"Stable identity key for a team after canonicalization.\"\"\"\n     return text_key(normalize_team(name))\n \n \n+# Generic club-type tokens ignored when computing a team's identity key, so\n+# \"Fortaleza\", \"Fortaleza FC\", and \"Fortaleza EC\" share one identity.\n+_GENERIC_TOKENS = {\"fc\", \"ec\", \"sc\", \"cf\", \"club\", \"clube\", \"esporte\",\n+                   \"futebol\"}\n+_GENERIC_PAIRS = {\"esporte clube\", \"futebol clube\", \"sport club\"}\n+\n+\n+def identity_key(name: str) -> str:\n+    \"\"\"Identity used for cross-file dedup and team matching.\n+\n+    Same as team_key but additionally ignores generic club-type tokens:\n+    \"Fortaleza FC\" and \"Fortaleza\" share the identity \"fortaleza\".\n+    \"\"\"\n+    tokens = team_key(name).split()\n+    while len(tokens) >= 2 and \" \".join(tokens[-2:]) in _GENERIC_PAIRS:\n+        del tokens[-2:]\n+    while tokens and tokens[-1] in _GENERIC_TOKENS:\n+        tokens.pop()\n+    while tokens and tokens[0] in _GENERIC_TOKENS:\n+        tokens.pop(0)\n+    return \" \".join(tokens)\n+\n+\n def derby_name(team_a: str, team_b: str) -> str | None:\n     \"\"\"Return the rivalry name if two teams are a known derby, else None.\"\"\"\n     a, b = normalize_team(team_a), normalize_team(team_b)\n     pair = (a, b) if a < b else (b, a)\n","additions":23,"deletions":0},"truncated":false},"title":"brazilian_soccer_mcp/normalize.py","time":{"start":1788227860862,"end":1788227860864}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Very informative! The collisions are almost all **harmless** — they're the same club written with/without accents (e.g., 'Gremio'/'Grêmio', 'Confianca'/'Confiança') — those SHOULD merge, and my text_key-based identity already merges them (ident uses text_key which strips accents — wait, but they show as separate display names in the same identity group: e.g. identity \"gremio\" contains displays 'Gremio' and 'Grêmio'. The identity key merges them ✓ — the collision listing is by *display name* but identity is the key. For dedup, matching happens on identity key, so 'Gremio'/'Grêmio' → same identity \"gremio\" ✓ — they'd be one node for dedup, though two display names could appear. Hmm — display inconsistency: matches loaded before alias fix would show \"Gremio\" while others show \"Grêmio\". Better: canonical display should be unique per identity. I should add accents-preserving aliases for these common ones. The alias table already covers Grêmio, Confiança? No — \"confianca\" not aliased. Both \"Confianca\" and \"Confiança\" → identity \"confianca\" but displays differ. To have ONE display per identity, I should map display → most common/canonical form. Simplest robust approach: after loading, build identity → display by picking the display with the most occurrences in the corpus (or first by priority). Then re-normalize... \n\nCleaner architecture: two-stage canonicalization in Dataset:\n1. normalize each raw name → `normalize_team` (aliases + suffix strip) — deterministic, no corpus knowledge.\n2. After collecting all matches, build identity groups (identity_key). For each identity, choose canonical display = the longest/most-accented? Choose by simple heuristic: prefer the display form that appears in an explicit canonical list; else prefer the form with accents (most \"correct\" Portuguese)? Simpler: pick the most frequent form, ties → lexicographic. Then REWRITE all matches' home/away display names to the chosen canonical, keeping _team_index consistent.\n\nThat guarantees: one display per identity, dedup on identity, and consistent queries. \n\nThe genuinely problematic collisions:\n- \"fortaleza\": ['Fortaleza', 'Fortaleza EC', 'Fortaleza FC'] — same club ✓ merge OK. (Fortaleza EC = Fortaleza Esporte Clube = Fortaleza ✓)\n- \"santa cruz\": ['Santa Cruz', 'Santa Cruz FC'] — Santa Cruz FC vs Santa Cruz (PE)?? There's also \"Santa Cruz-RN\"/\"Santa Cruz-RS\" aliased separately ✓. \"Santa Cruz FC\" (from BR — probably Santa Cruz FC de Recife? or Minas?) — merging with Santa Cruz is acceptable-ish. Hmm, there IS \"Santa Cruz FC\" (MG)? Risk is minor for demo.\n- \"vitoria\": ['Vitoria EC', 'Vitória'] — EC Vitória = Vitória ✓ same club.\n- \"brasil\": \"brasilia\": ['Brasilia FC', 'Brasília'] — same club (Brasília FC? Brasília = capital club; \"Brasilia FC\"... merge OK).\n- \"real\": ['Real', 'Real FC'] — ambiguous (Real-AM? Real Noroeste-ES shows separately as \"real noroeste\"). Merge OK.\n- \"atletico\": ['Atletico', 'Atlético'] — same ambiguity either way; merging them is fine (same unknown club).\n- \"moto\": ['Moto Club', 'Moto Clube'] — same club ✓.\n- \"river\": ['River', 'Ríver'] — \"Ríver\" (Piauí) vs \"River\" (River-PI?) — likely same club (Ríver AC). OK.\n- Everything else is same-club spelling variance.\n\nDangerous ones? \"operario\": ['Operario', 'Operário'] — but there are THREE Operários (PR, MS, MT) that keep state suffixes (\"Operario PR\" etc.) — bare \"Operario\"/\"Operário\" in BR data is probably Operário-PR... merging them with each other is what happens today anyway (both bare). Fine.\n\nSo the plan:\n1. Add `identity_key()` to normalize.py: text_key(normalize) + strip trailing generic tokens {fc, ec, sc, cf, club, clube, esporte, futebol} and trailing 2-token combos (\"esporte clube\", \"futebol clube\", \"sport club\", \"moto club\"? no). From the collision list, stripping worked as intended (identities like \"fortaleza\" merged 3 forms; no over-merge evident except possibly \"santa cruz\"). I'll also strip leading \"fc \" (\"FC Cascavel\" vs \"Fc Cascavel\" — same display anyway).\n   - Note my earlier concern: dropping \"ac\" — my strip list above didn't include \"ac\" and collisions look fine. Keep \"ac\" out (Rio Branco AC / River AC / Galvez AC stay distinct). Wait \"river\": \"River\"/\"Ríver\" merged — that's fine.\n   - Careful: identity strip of \"sc\" — \"Central SC\" → \"central\" ✓ same club as \"Central\"? There are two Central SCs (PE)... whatever, same data source.\n   - \"ec\": \"Vitoria EC\" → \"vitoria\" = \"Vitória\" ✓ good (that fixes \"Vitória\" display to have ONE identity).\n2. Add `identity_key` usage: Dataset dedup keys use identity_key; _team_index keyed by identity? Hmm — queries resolve team → display name. If _team_index is keyed by display but multiple displays share identity, matching \"Fortaleza EC\" → normalize → \"Fortaleza EC\" → not in index (index has \"Fortaleza\"). Fix: canonicalize displays post-load so index keys are the canonical display of each identity, and `resolve_team` maps any input → identity → canonical display via an identity→display map built at load.\n\nImplementation in Dataset:\n- After loading matches: build `self._identity_to_display: dict[str, str]` by counting display frequency per identity (via Counter over all match team occurrences) — pick most common (ties: shortest? lexicographic smallest for determinism). Prefer accented/canonical forms: frequency should handle it since \"Fortaleza\" appears in multiple files.\n- Then rewrite match.home_team/away_team to canonical display (dataclasses are mutable ✓), rebuild _team_index keyed by canonical display.\n- `resolve_team(name)`: display = identity_to_display.get(identity_key(name)) or normalize_team(name).\n\nBut wait — Dataset currently rewrites? Match dataclass has plain fields ✓ mutable.\n\nAlso player clubs: FIFA clubs like \"Grêmio\" — identity for players isn't merged with match teams necessarily; get_club_overview uses text_key equality on club vs team display. With canonical displays (\"Grêmio\"), FIFA \"Grêmio\" matches text_key ✓. \"Ceará Sporting Club\" vs \"Ceará\" — overview lookup: club_key \"ceara sporting clube\"? text_key(\"Ceará\") = \"ceara\" ≠ \"ceara sporting club\" → fuzzy contains works (club_key in player_club_key → \"ceara\" in \"ceara sporting clube\" ✓ gets caught by third fallback). Improve get_club_overview to use identity_key on both sides! identity_key(\"Ceará Sporting Club\") = \"ceara sporting\"? hmm — strip \"sporting club\"? not in strip list. \"ceara sporting clube\"... Let me add \"sporting club\"/\"sport club\"/\"sporting clube\" to strip list? \"Sport Recife\" = \"Sport Club do Recife\" — alias handles. For club overview I'll do: compare identity_key(team) with identity_key(player.club) — \"ceara sporting clube\" strip trailing \"clube\" → \"ceara sporting\" ≠ \"ceara\". Eh. Add explicit aliases instead: \"ceara sporting clube\" → \"Ceará\" ✓ already there! identity_key of alias result \"Ceará\" → \"ceara\" ✓. And \"america fc minas gerais\" → aliased ✓. \"sport club do recife\" → aliased ✓. So identity_key(aliased display) works when aliases exist. For unknown clubs, fallback fuzzy in overview remains.\n\n3. Also need display-side aliases for accent variants seen in the corpus where NO alias exists (e.g., 'Tubarao'/'Tubarão' → identity \"tubarao\" merges ✓, display picked by frequency — \"Tubarão\" likely from cup file \"Tubarão - SC\"? both appear; frequency picks one; fine).\n\nHmm wait, one more consideration: identity merging 'Gremio' (Brasileirao) vs 'Grêmio' — alias \"gremio\" → \"Grêmio\" exists ✓ so normalize already handles.\n\n4. For standings correctness, dedup must use identity-based team names — after the rewrite step. So order: load all → canonicalize displays → THEN dedup? Currently dedup happens during loading (before canonicalization). \"Fortaleza FC\" vs \"Fortaleza\" dedup would fail even after alias... no wait, with identity dedup I should dedup AFTER canonicalization. Restructure: `_add_match` just appends; after loading all sources, run `_canonicalize_teams()` then `_deduplicate()` then sort + index. Dedup after canonicalization: key = (competition, season, date, identity(home), identity(away)) → but display may differ; keep first occurrence by source priority. Source priority for keep: prefer novo (has arena) over Brasileirao over BR? Actually Brasileirao_Matches has round info + states; novo has arena+states+round. Prefer order: novo > Brasileirao > cup > lib > BR? For Copa do Brasil: cup file > BR. Hmm — simpler: dedup keep-first-by-load-order with load order: novo first, Brasileirao second, cup, lib, BR last. novo covers 2003-2019 (has arena), Brasileirao 2012-2022 (has round, states). Overlap 2012-2019: keeping novo loses nothing (round present? novo has Rodada ✓, states ✓, arena ✓; Brasileirao adds nothing except different datetime). Then BR Serie A/Copa dedup against the union.\n\nWait, one wrinkle: novo \"Vencedor\" column redundant with goals ✓.\n\n5. Also for dedup: matches with goals=None (82 in Brasileirao 2020?) — the 82 empty-goal rows are 2020 matches? COVID-era... whatever; dedup key includes goals-None handling: if goals None, still dedup on same key? If one source has goals and other doesn't for same match, prefer the one WITH goals. Implement: group by key, prefer record with goals.\n\nSimpler robust dedup: iterate matches in priority order; for each, compute key; if key seen → skip. Also compute key for goals-None matches (date may still exist). All the None-goal rows presumably only exist in Brasileirao_Matches (82) — likely postponed matches not present in other files → kept as schedule entries ✓.\n\n6. While at it: BR \"Serie A\" merge to \"Brasileirão\" — is that right for standings? BR Serie A 2014-2023; Brasileirao_Matches 2012-2022; novo 2003-2019. After dedup: e.g. 2014 season = novo + Brasileirao + BR overlapping → deduped to ~380 ✓. 2023 = BR only (380?) → standings possible for 2023 ✓ (spec asks \"Which team scored the most goals in Serie A 2023\" ✓).\n\nBut wait — is BR Serie A actually the Brasileirão Serie A? tournaments: Serie A/B/C ✓ yes.\n\nCopa do Brasil BR + cup file dedup ✓.\n\n7. Player club canonicalization: not needed beyond aliases.\n\nLet me now restructure data_loader.py accordingly and add identity_key + a few more aliases to normalize.py. Also add aliases discovered: \"fortaleza fc\" (identity handles), \"ec juventude\" (identity handles: \"ec juventude\" strip trailing \"ec\"? \"juventude ec\"? token order — \"ec juventude\" has EC LEADING. My strip only handles trailing tokens! \"EC Juventude\" → key \"ec juventude\" — leading \"ec\" not stripped → identity \"ec juventude\" ≠ \"juventude\". Add leading-token strip too: strip leading \"fc\"/\"ec\"/\"sc\"/\"ac\"/\"cf\"? \"FC Cascavel\" → \"cascavel\" ✓ harmless. \"EC Vitória\"? appears as \"Vitoria EC\" trailing ✓. Leading strip: \"EC Juventude\" → \"juventude\" ✓. Any real club named \"EC X\" where \"X\" alone is another club? Unlikely in corpus. I'll strip leading generic tokens too.\n\nAfter canonical display selection, dedup, the \"Vencedor\" etc. Let me also double-check identity strip doesn't over-merge: 'Gremio' → \"gremio\" ✓; 'Guarani' (SP) vs 'Guaraní' (PAR) — both → identity \"guarani\"!! COLLISION DANGER: Brazilian Guarani (Campinas, appears in novo/Brasileirao Serie A 2003-2007?) vs Paraguayan Guaraní (Libertadores). If they merge, Libertadores \"Guaraní\" matches would share identity with Brazilian Guarani. Dedup across competitions is keyed by (competition, season, date, home, away) — competitions differ (\"Libertadores\" vs \"Brasileirão\") → no wrong dedup. Team queries: \"Guarani\" would return matches from both — mildly wrong but acceptable? Hmm, better to fix: alias \"guarani\" (bare) → \"Guarani\" and \"guarani par\" → \"Guaraní\" — but identity strips to same key anyway. I need identity to NOT merge these: guard: strip trailing tokens ONLY if result not empty and... I can't know. Alternative: exclude specific collisions by NOT stripping when name has a country suffix already processed...\n\nPragmatic: for Libertadores-only teams, name collisions with Brazilian clubs: \"Guaraní\" (PAR) vs \"Guarani\" (SP), \"Nacional\" (URU) vs \"Nacional-AM\" (aliased distinct ✓ since alias applied before strip: \"nacional am\" → display \"Nacional-AM\" → identity \"nacional am\"? strip trailing \"am\"? \"am\" is not in strip list ✓ stays distinct). \"guaraní\": I'll add alias \"guarani\" → \"Guarani\" and set identity of \"Guaraní\" — can't fix via alias since both alias to different displays but identity strips both to \"guarani\".\n\nOption: make identity smarter — keep 2-char state/country codes? \"Guaraní-PAR\" normalizes to \"Guaraní\" (suffix stripped, full-key alias \"guarani par\" → \"Guaraní\") — display \"Guaraní\". identity_key(\"Guaraní\") → \"guarani\". identity_key(\"Guarani\") → \"guarani\". Merge happens. To disambiguate, alias \"guaraní\" display → identity \"guarani par\"? I could make identity_key check a hard-coded identity overrides map: {\"guaraní\": \"guarani par\", ...}. Add `IDENTITY_OVERRIDES = {\"guarani\": \"guarani\", \"guaraní→\"...\"}`. Since identity_key works on normalized display: override map: display_key \"guarani\" → \"guarani sp\"? no — bare \"Guarani\" IS the Brazilian club (SP). Override: identity_key(\"Guaraní\") — how do I know it's the Paraguayan one vs Brazilian misspelling without accents? \"Guaraní\" with accent = Paraguayan in Libertadores data; \"Guarani\" bare = Brazilian. So override map on the normalized display: {\"guarani\": \"guarani\", \"guaraní\" (accents PRESERVED here?)} — identity_key currently accent-strips. I can add a special-case: after computing key, check map `IDENTITY_HINTS = {\"guarani\": \"guarani-par\" if original had accents...}` — getting silly.\n\nReality check: how often does \"Guaraní\" (PAR) matter? Libertadores matches. How often \"Guarani\" (SP)? Serie A 2003-2008 in novo + Serie B. If merged, head-to-head/standings? Standings use competition filter — Serie A standings would include \"Guaraní\" if it played Libertadores matches?? No — standings filter competition=\"Brasileirão\" so Libertadores Guaraní matches excluded ✓. Team queries \"Guarani\" would show Libertadores matches mixed in — minor demo imprecision. H2H \"Guarani vs someone\" — minor.\n\nDecision: accept the merge for \"guarani\" (document in alias comments). The data-quality benefit (Fortaleza FC/EC Juventude dedup) far outweighs. But wait — display canonicalization picks ONE display per identity — if \"Guaraní\" (PAR) wins by frequency... frequencies: Guarani (SP) appears in ~180 Serie A matches (novo+Brasileirao? Brasileirao 2012+ doesn't have Guarani; novo has maybe 100+); Guaraní PAR in Libertadores ~20. Brazilian wins → Libertadores rows will display \"Guarani\" for the Paraguayan club. Acceptable for demo; the source row is preserved.\n\nSimilarly \"Nacional\": alias \"nacional\" → \"Nacional\" and \"nacional uru\" → \"Nacional\" ✓ both merge to identity \"nacional\" — intended (same Uruguayan club). \"Nacional-AM\" → alias → display \"Nacional-AM\" → identity \"nacional am\" ✓ distinct.\n\nOK also \"River\" (PI \"Ríver\") vs River Plate?? \"River Plate\" not in corpus (Libertadores 2013+ hmm \"River Plate\" would appear 2015+... earlier scan of Libertadores teams showed up to 'La Guaira' — let me trust aliases; if \"River Plate\" exists it stays \"River Plate\" identity \"river plate\" ≠ \"river\" ✓.\n\n8. One more: \"vasco da gama\" strip trailing \"gama\"? \"gama\" not in strip list ✓. \"da\" not stripped ✓ (only listed tokens stripped).\n\nCareful with strip list: tokens {\"fc\",\"ec\",\"sc\",\"cf\",\"club\",\"clube\",\"esporte\",\"futebol\",\"sporting\",\"sport club\"?}. \"Sport Recife\" → identity \"sport recife\"? trailing \"recife\" not stripped ✓. But wait — \"Sport Club do Recife\" aliased → \"Sport Recife\" ✓. \"Sporting Cristal\"? not in corpus. I'll NOT include \"sporting\"/\"sport\" tokens. Final strip tokens: {\"fc\", \"ec\", \"sc\", \"cf\", \"club\", \"clube\", \"esporte\", \"futebol\"} as single trailing/leading tokens, plus 2-token combos {\"esporte clube\", \"futebol clube\", \"sport club\"}.\n\nWait \"sc\" trailing: \"Criciuma SC\"? alias \"criciuma\" ✓ first. \"Avai-SC\"? suffix strip already handles state codes. \"SC\" as club-type: \"Central SC\" → \"central\" ✓. Hmm \"sc\" strip risk: \"SC Internacional\"? not in corpus (\"Internacional\" plain). OK include.\n\nAlso \"cf\": \"SC Corinthians Paulista\"? not in corpus. Fine.\n\nNow — one more thing to verify: BR Serie A 2022 \"Fortaleza FC\" — after identity strip \"fortaleza\" ✓ merge with Brasileirao \"Fortaleza\". But display canonicalization: which display wins — \"Fortaleza\" (frequent) ✓.\n\nLet me now rewrite normalize.py additions (identity_key) and rework data_loader (canonicalize → dedup → index). Also update resolve_team to use identity map.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05aafb96c0011IuKcYwR1IkH19","sessionID":"ses_fa566fb65ffeJ8k4Q5FXGIEPJE","messageID":"msg_05aad4a2d00192mUyEX4PXb7Ic"}}
